###Pure
// position-keeping -- WHAT A FIRM OWNS, PER ACCOUNT, PER INSTRUMENT, PER DAY.
//
// Layer 1. Depends on core-instrument (what the thing is) and core-account (whose it is),
// and on nothing else.
//
// Two shapes drive the whole project.
//
//   COMPOSITE KEY  A position is (account, instrument, cob date) and is not identified by
//                  anything shorter. The same instrument in the same account on two dates
//                  is TWO positions, not one row that moves -- that is the entire reason
//                  position keeping exists, because yesterday's close is what today's open
//                  is reconciled against and it must still be there to look at. And
//                  because core-account keys an account by (institutionId, accountNo), the
//                  account half of that key is TWO columns, not one: a position carries
//                  INSTITUTION_ID and ACCOUNT_NO and the join to CA_ACCOUNT matches both.
//                  So the grain of PK_POSITION is FOUR columns, and every child record --
//                  movement, lot, adjustment, ladder entry, break -- carries all four plus
//                  its own discriminator.
//
//   AGGREGATION    Four of the classes below are not events. They are per-group TOTALS:
//                  one row per account per day, one row per instrument per day, one row
//                  per position per day summarising its movements, and one row per
//                  account-instrument pair over its whole life. Each is mapped onto a
//                  store View whose ~groupBy IS its primary key, so an instance of
//                  AccountDayTotal is a GROUP and never an event. This is what a
//                  statement, a firm-wide inventory check and a holding history actually
//                  read, and reading them does not pull the underlying position rows.
//
// Nothing here is milestoned. A position is already dated by its own key; layering
// business time on top of COB_DATE would be two answers to one question.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the grain

// THE POSITION. What one account held in one instrument at the close of one business
// day: how much it opened with, how much it closed with, what that stock cost, and what
// has and has not yet been earned on it. This class is the root of the project and the
// thing every downstream project joins to.
Class position_keeping::Position
{
   // The key, and all four parts of it are required. institutionId + accountNo is the
   // account (core-account's key, carried whole); instrumentId is core-instrument's key;
   // cobDate is the close-of-business date this row states the holding as at.
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];

   // Quantity. openingQuantity is yesterday's closingQuantity, copied forward by the run
   // rather than recomputed, so a break between the two is visible instead of silently
   // corrected. settledQuantity is what has actually settled; pendingQuantity is what is
   // traded but not yet delivered, and the two need not sum to closing on a fail.
   openingQuantity: Float[1];
   closingQuantity: Float[1];
   settledQuantity: Float[1];
   pendingQuantity: Float[0..1];

   // Cost. averageCost is the weighted average unit cost of the stock still held, in
   // costCurrency (the instrument's currency, not the account's).
   averageCost: Float[1];
   costCurrency: String[1];
   costBasisAmount: Float[1];

   // Value and P&L. realisedPnl is what closing out has already crystallised on this
   // instrument in this account up to this cob date; unrealisedPnl is what the remaining
   // stock would crystallise at marketPrice. They are separate because tax and accounting
   // treat them differently, and because only one of them changes when nothing is traded.
   marketPrice: Float[0..1];
   marketValue: Float[0..1];
   realisedPnl: Float[1];
   unrealisedPnl: Float[1];
   pnlCurrency: String[1];

   positionStatus: String[1];
   isShort: Boolean[1];
   lastMovementDate: StrictDate[0..1];

   // The batch that produced this row, so a position can always be traced to a run.
   runSeq: Integer[1];

   // CROSS-PROJECT. Both ends of the key resolve to another project's class.
   account: core_account::Account[1];
   instrument: core_instrument::Instrument[1];

   run: position_keeping::PositionRun[1];

   movements: position_keeping::PositionMovement[*];
   lots: position_keeping::PositionLot[*];
   adjustments: position_keeping::PositionAdjustment[*];
   ladder: position_keeping::SettlementLadderEntry[*];
   breaks: position_keeping::PositionBreak[*];

   // The aggregates this position rolls up into. Navigating any of these crosses into a
   // GROUP BY and never into another position row.
   movementTotal: position_keeping::MovementTotal[0..1];
   accountDayTotal: position_keeping::AccountDayTotal[0..1];
   instrumentDayTotal: position_keeping::InstrumentDayTotal[0..1];
   lifetime: position_keeping::AccountInstrumentLifetime[0..1];

   // The four-part identity as one string, for messages that must name a position
   // without carrying four fields.
   positionKey() { $this.institutionId + '/' + $this.accountNo + '/' + $this.instrumentId } : String[1];

   // What the day did to the holding. Signed: negative is a net sale.
   quantityChange() { $this.closingQuantity - $this.openingQuantity } : Float[1];

   // Total P&L is only ever the sum of the two halves; nothing stores it, because a
   // stored total is one more thing that can disagree with its parts.
   totalPnl() { $this.realisedPnl + $this.unrealisedPnl } : Float[1];

   // Traded but undelivered. A non-zero value on an old cob date is a settlement fail.
   unsettledQuantity() { $this.closingQuantity - $this.settledQuantity } : Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ what moved it

// ONE THING THAT CHANGED A POSITION during its cob date -- a buy, a sale, a transfer in,
// a corporate action entitlement. Keyed by the position's four columns plus a sequence,
// because two buys of the same instrument in the same account on the same day are two
// movements and must not collapse into one.
Class position_keeping::PositionMovement
{
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   movementSeq: Integer[1];

   movementType: String[1];
   tradeReference: String[0..1];
   quantityDelta: Float[1];
   signedCash: Float[1];
   price: Float[0..1];
   settlementDate: StrictDate[0..1];
   sourceSystem: String[1];

   position: position_keeping::Position[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ cost, lot by lot

// ONE PARCEL OF STOCK BOUGHT AT ONE PRICE, still open or closed on this cob date. The
// average cost on the position is a summary; this is the detail it is computed from, and
// it is what a realised-P&L calculation actually consumes, because which lot you sell
// decides what you realise. Keyed by the position's four columns plus the lot id.
Class position_keeping::PositionLot
{
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   lotId: String[1];

   openDate: StrictDate[1];
   openQuantity: Float[1];
   remainingQuantity: Float[1];
   unitCost: Float[1];
   lotCurrency: String[1];
   closedOn: StrictDate[0..1];

   position: position_keeping::Position[1];
   closures: position_keeping::LotClosure[*];

   // A lot is open until it is fully consumed. Absence of a close date is the only
   // honest test -- a lot can be reopened by a cancellation.
   isOpen() { $this.closedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];

   // What is left of the parcel, at what it cost.
   remainingCost() { $this.remainingQuantity * $this.unitCost } : Float[1];
}

// ONE MATCH OF A SALE AGAINST ONE LOT -- the event that turns unrealised P&L into
// realised P&L. Keyed by the lot's five columns plus a sequence, because one sale can eat
// several lots and one lot can be eaten by several sales. matchingMethod records WHICH
// rule chose this lot (FIFO, LIFO, HIFO, AVERAGE), since the realised amount is only
// defensible with it.
Class position_keeping::LotClosure
{
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   lotId: String[1];
   closureSeq: Integer[1];

   closedQuantity: Float[1];
   closePrice: Float[1];
   realisedAmount: Float[1];
   matchingMethod: String[1];
   movementSeq: Integer[0..1];

   lot: position_keeping::PositionLot[1];
}

// A DELIBERATE OVERRIDE OF A COMPUTED POSITION, booked by a human and approved by another
// one. Kept as its own row rather than folded into the position, so the computed figure
// and the correction are both still readable afterwards. Keyed by the position's four
// columns plus a sequence.
Class position_keeping::PositionAdjustment
{
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   adjustmentSeq: Integer[1];

   adjustmentType: String[1];
   quantityDelta: Float[0..1];
   amountDelta: Float[0..1];
   reason: String[1];
   raisedBy: String[1];
   approvedBy: String[0..1];
   raisedOn: StrictDate[1];

   position: position_keeping::Position[1];

   // Nobody's own adjustment counts until someone else signs it.
   isApproved() { $this.approvedBy->isEmpty()->not() } : Boolean[1];
}

// WHAT IS STILL EXPECTED TO SETTLE OUT OF THIS POSITION, ON ONE FUTURE DATE. Keyed by the
// position's four columns plus the settlement date: a position on one cob date can have
// deliveries due on several later dates, and each is its own row. This is the shape a
// funding or a fails report reads.
Class position_keeping::SettlementLadderEntry
{
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   settlementDate: StrictDate[1];

   expectedQuantity: Float[1];
   expectedCash: Float[1];
   ladderCurrency: String[1];
   isOverdue: Boolean[1];

   position: position_keeping::Position[1];
}

// A DISAGREEMENT ABOUT A POSITION that somebody has to resolve -- opening quantity not
// equal to yesterday's close, movements not summing to the day's change, custodian saying
// something else. Keyed by the position's four columns plus the break code, so the same
// kind of break cannot be raised twice on one position.
Class position_keeping::PositionBreak
{
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   breakCode: String[1];

   breakQuantity: Float[0..1];
   breakAmount: Float[0..1];
   severity: String[1];
   detectedOn: StrictDate[1];
   resolvedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   assignedTo: String[0..1];

   position: position_keeping::Position[1];

   // Open until it is resolved. Same reasoning as an account restriction upstream: the
   // history stays, so only the absence of a resolution date means still open.
   isOpen() { $this.resolvedOn->isEmpty() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ONE EXECUTION OF THE POSITION-KEEPING BATCH for one cob date. Keyed by (cobDate,
// runSeq) because a day can be run more than once -- an intraday estimate, a close, then
// a rerun after a late trade -- and every position row names the run that made it.
Class position_keeping::PositionRun
{
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   runSeq: Integer[1];

   runType: String[1];
   runStatus: String[1];
   startedOn: StrictDate[1];
   completedOn: StrictDate[0..1];
   positionCount: Integer[0..1];
   triggeredBy: String[1];

   positions: position_keeping::Position[*];

   // A run that has not finished has not produced a usable set of positions.
   isComplete() { $this.completedOn->isEmpty()->not() } : Boolean[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ the aggregates
//
// The four classes below are mapped onto store Views. Their rows are GROUPS, not events:
// a query for one of them makes the engine fold a GROUP BY into its SQL rather than read
// the position rows and total them in memory. Note what a group does NOT produce -- an
// account with no positions on a date forms no group and is simply absent, rather than
// appearing with a total of zero.

// ONE ACCOUNT'S WHOLE BOOK ON ONE DAY, as one row. This is what the top of a client
// statement says: how many lines, what they are worth, what has been made on them. The
// grouping is (institutionId, accountNo, cobDate) -- the account key is two columns here
// as everywhere else in this project.
Class position_keeping::AccountDayTotal
{
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];

   positionCount: Integer[1];
   totalMarketValue: Float[0..1];
   totalCostBasis: Float[1];
   totalRealisedPnl: Float[1];
   totalUnrealisedPnl: Float[1];

   account: core_account::Account[1];
   positions: position_keeping::Position[*];

   totalPnl() { $this.totalRealisedPnl + $this.totalUnrealisedPnl } : Float[1];

   // Division widens to Float even when both sides are Integer, so this is Float[1] and
   // could not be Integer[1] whatever the operands were.
   averageLineValue() { $this.totalCostBasis / $this.positionCount } : Float[1];
}

// ONE INSTRUMENT ACROSS EVERY ACCOUNT ON ONE DAY, as one row: the firm's net inventory in
// that security. The grouping is (instrumentId, cobDate) and deliberately drops the
// account, which is exactly the question a stock-record or an issuer-concentration check
// asks and the position table cannot answer without scanning.
Class position_keeping::InstrumentDayTotal
{
   instrumentId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];

   accountCount: Integer[1];
   netQuantity: Float[1];
   longestQuantity: Float[0..1];
   totalMarketValue: Float[0..1];

   instrument: core_instrument::Instrument[1];
   positions: position_keeping::Position[*];
}

// ONE POSITION'S DAY OF MOVEMENTS, TOTALLED. Same four-column grain as the position
// itself, but built by grouping the movement rows -- so comparing this against the
// position's own quantityChange() is the reconciliation that raises a break, and it costs
// one grouped read instead of fetching every movement.
Class position_keeping::MovementTotal
{
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];

   movementCount: Integer[1];
   netQuantityDelta: Float[1];
   netCash: Float[1];

   position: position_keeping::Position[1];
}

// ONE ACCOUNT'S WHOLE HISTORY IN ONE INSTRUMENT, as one row. The grouping drops the cob
// date -- the one thing every other key in this project keeps -- which is what makes it
// a holding history rather than a position: first and last day held, how many days, and
// everything ever realised on it.
Class position_keeping::AccountInstrumentLifetime
{
   institutionId: String[1];
   accountNo: String[1];
   instrumentId: String[1];

   dayCount: Integer[1];
   firstCobDate: StrictDate[1];
   lastCobDate: StrictDate[1];
   lifetimeRealisedPnl: Float[1];

   account: core_account::Account[1];
   instrument: core_instrument::Instrument[1];
   positions: position_keeping::Position[*];
}
